description:
Define cofferdam_core::Language as the abstraction the platform pivots on, then make Engine and CheckContext generic over it. Source: design/platform-extensibility.md.

WHY: with cofferdam-ts extracted, the next step is generifying the engine so a second language adapter slots in without touching core or engine. Today there will be exactly one impl, cofferdam_ts::TypeScript; the CLI binary wires it in. Tomorrow cofferdam_py::Python coexists with no platform changes.

PROPOSED SHAPE (per design doc):

  pub trait Language: 'static {
      type Ast: Send + Sync;
      fn name() -> &'static str;
      fn default_extensions() -> &'static [&'static str];
      fn parse(source: &str) -> Result<Self::Ast, ParseError>;
  }

  pub struct Engine<L: Language> { /* ... */ }
  pub trait Check<L: Language>: Send + Sync {
      fn meta(&self) -> &'static CheckMeta;
      fn run(&self, file: &SourceFile, ctx: &mut CheckContext<'_, L>) -> Vec<Issue>;
      fn finalize(&self, _ctx: &mut FinalizeContext<'_>) -> Vec<Issue> { vec![] }
  }

ACCEPTANCE:
- cofferdam_core::Language trait exists with Ast assoc type + parse / name / default_extensions.
- Engine<L: Language>, Check<L>, CheckContext<'_, L> are generic.
- cofferdam_ts implements Language.
- cofferdam-cli wires TypeScript in at the binary level.
- A platform-purity doc test in cofferdam-core demonstrates building a Check, running it, and emitting an Issue WITHOUT pulling cofferdam-ts. If the test ever needs a TS dep, the abstraction has eroded.

OPEN QUESTION (decision #6 in the design): should Language know about line endings, encoding, BOMs? Recommendation in the doc: pull byte/line/column logic fully into core; every language has the same problems with CRLF and BOM.

PARENT: cd-81a (epic)
DEPENDS ON: cd-8wj (crate split must land first — Language can't sit on top of an oxc-bound core)
